From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alsey Coleman Miller <alseycmiller@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 00:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Use the 64-bit info type wherever the CF info word is 64-bit

CFRuntimeBase's _cfinfoa is a 64-bit atomic on 64-bit targets and in
every DEPLOYMENT_RUNTIME_SWIFT build, but __CFInfoType, which the info
accessors read it into, is 64 bits only on 64-bit targets and Android.
On other 32-bit targets __CFRuntimeSetValue's compare-exchange loop
passes a 4-byte expected value to an 8-byte exchange, which compares
and writes back the 4 bytes after it as well. On 32-bit PowerPC the
loop never succeeds, and the first CFArray hangs.

Use the 64-bit type wherever the word is 64-bit, as Android already
does. The info bits all sit in the low 32, so no value changes.
---
--- a/Sources/CoreFoundation/internalInclude/CFInternal.h
+++ b/Sources/CoreFoundation/internalInclude/CFInternal.h
@@ -282,7 +282,11 @@
 #define __CFBitfield64GetValue(V, N1, N2)	(((V) & __CFBitfield64Mask(N1, N2)) >> (N2))
 #define __CFBitfield64SetValue(V, N1, N2, X)	((V) = ((V) & ~__CFBitfield64Mask(N1, N2)) | ((((uint64_t)X) << (N2)) & __CFBitfield64Mask(N1, N2)))
 
-#if TARGET_RT_64_BIT || TARGET_OS_ANDROID
+// The info word is 64 bits wide on 64-bit targets and wherever CF runs under
+// Swift (see CFRuntimeBase). Reading it into a narrower type truncates it, and
+// passing a narrower expected value to the compare-exchange loops below makes
+// them compare against bytes that are not part of it.
+#if TARGET_RT_64_BIT || TARGET_OS_ANDROID || DEPLOYMENT_RUNTIME_SWIFT
 typedef uint64_t __CFInfoType;
 #define __CFInfoMask(N1, N2) __CFBitfield64Mask(N1, N2)
 #else
